Test the literal seam only: the target's first characters must complete a lexical word begun at the genuine predecessor's end, including Chinese compound words and line-end hyphenation. A whole new word, a later word inside the target, or a merely related phrase does not establish this fault. Ignore syntax and paragraph-style issues.
{% else %}
Does this proposed paragraph break preserve whole words? Check the tail of the real predecessor against the target opening for a word split across the seam, including Chinese multi-character words and words broken by a page transition. Ignore other sentence-level and paragraph-style issues in this question.
